UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 11, Lesson 1: The five food groups', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and pasta, mostly wholegrain; lean meat, poultry, fish, eggs, tofu, nuts, seeds and legumes; and milk, yoghurt, cheese or alternatives. Each group gives the body different nutrients, so eating a variety from all five groups supports growth, energy and health.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort these foods into groups: carrots, brown rice, scrambled eggs, banana, cheese. Name the group for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCarrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each food goes in the group that matches the nutrients it gives.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nList everything you ate yesterday and sort it into the five groups. Mark any group that was missing and suggest two foods you could add tomorrow. Families eat different foods for cultural, religious, medical or allergy reasons, so choose foods that suit yours and share only what you like.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and pasta, mostly wholegrain; lean meat, poultry, fish, eggs, tofu, nuts, seeds and legumes; and milk, yoghurt, cheese or alternatives. Each group gives the body different nutrients, so eating a variety from all five groups supports growth, energy and health.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: List everything you ate yesterday and sort it into the five groups. Mark any group that was missing and suggest two foods you could add tomorrow. Families eat different foods for cultural, religious, medical or allergy r Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each food goes in the group that matches the… Reasoning: I used the five food groups by attending to variety rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. Correction: Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each… Why: the correction restores accurate use of the five food groups.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating the five food groups on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with the five food groups, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each… This matches the lesson skill for the five food groups.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each…","Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients."],"question":"Sort these foods into groups: carrots, brown rice, scrambled eggs, banana, cheese. Name the group for each. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of variety with clear evidence.","Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients.","The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and…","Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named variety, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the variety used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising the five food groups. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about the five food groups.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about variety?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lean meat group:…","Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit;… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["List everything you ate yesterday and sort it into the five groups. Mark any group that was missing and suggest two foods you could add tomorrow.…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating the five food groups on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each food goes in the group that matches the… Reasoning: I used the five food groups by attending to variety rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with the five food groups, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. Correction: Carrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each… Why: the correction restores accurate use of the five food groups.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w11-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise the five food groups.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use the five food groups with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify variety and explain why it matters.","I can practise nutrients independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a food group needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and pasta, mostly wholegrain; lean meat, poultry, fish, eggs, tofu, nuts, seeds and legumes; and milk, yoghurt, cheese or…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and pasta, mostly wholegrain; lean meat, poultry, fish, eggs, tofu, nuts, seeds and legumes; and milk, yoghurt, cheese or alternatives. Each group gives the body different nutrients, so eating a variety from all five groups supports growth, energy and health.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lean meat group: chicken. Vegetables: salad. Fruit: apple. Milk group: yoghurt. All five groups appear, so the lunch has variety.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort these foods into groups: carrots, brown rice, scrambled eggs, banana, cheese. Name the group for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCarrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each food goes in the group that matches the nutrients it gives.","explain":"The Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and pasta, mostly wholegrain; lean meat, poultry, fish, eggs, tofu, nuts, seeds and legumes; and milk, yoghurt, cheese or alternatives. Each group gives the body different nutrients, so eating a variety from all five groups supports growth, energy and health.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two of the five food groups.","caption":"Each group has a job.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Group 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">vegetables and legumes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Group 2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fruit</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lea Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lea Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lean meat group: chicken. Vegetables: salad. Fruit: apple. Milk group: yoghurt. All five groups appear, so the lunch has variety. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"The other food groups.","caption":"Variety from all five.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Group 3</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain foods</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Group 4 and 5</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">protein foods, milk foods</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lean meat group: chicken. Vegetables: salad. Fruit: apple. Milk group: yoghurt. All five groups appear, so the lunch has variety.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCarrots: vegetables. Brown rice: grain foods. Eggs: lean meat, eggs, tofu, nuts and legumes group. Banana: fruit. Cheese: milk, yoghurt and cheese group. Each food goes in the group that matches the nutrients it gives.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking one ''superfood'' can replace the other groups. Bodies need variety from all five groups, because different foods give different nutrients. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the five food groups?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe Australian Guide to Healthy Eating sorts foods into five groups: vegetables and legumes or beans; fruit; grain (cereal) foods such as bread, rice and pasta, mostly wholegrain; lean meat, poultry, fish, eggs, tofu, nuts, seeds and legumes; and milk, yoghurt, cheese or alternatives. Each group gives the body different nutrients, so eating a variety from all five groups supports growth, energy and health. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 11, Lesson 1: The five food groups","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":11,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a lunch of a wholegrain sandwich with chicken and salad, an apple and a small tub of yoghurt. Grain: bread. Lean meat group: chicken. Vegetables: salad. Fruit: apple. Milk group: yoghurt. All five groups appear, so the lunch has variety.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two of the five food groups.","caption":"Each group has a job.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Group 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">vegetables and legumes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Group 2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fruit</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"The other food groups.","caption":"Variety from all five.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Group 3</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain foods</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Group 4 and 5</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">protein foods, milk foods</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w11-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
